 # pypto-lib tools
 
+## Recommended Agent PGO Workflow
+
+The best current design is a two-level profiling workflow:
+
+1. Keep raw DFX artifacts for audit and human inspection.
+2. Give agents a structured, topology-preserving profile first.
+
+Raw `merged_swimlane_*.json` is a visual Perfetto trace. Raw `pmu.csv` is a
+counter table. Neither is the right first context for an optimizer. The agent
+should first read `agent_pgo_profile.json`, then fetch task rows or raw files
+only when the compact evidence is ambiguous.
+
+Recommended capture for candidate runs:
+
+```bash
+python <case>.py -p a2a3 -d 0 --enable-l2-swimlane --enable-pmu 2
+```
+
+When dependency criticality matters, capture `deps.json` once per stable task
+topology and reuse it with later profiling runs:
+
+```bash
+python <case>.py -p a2a3 -d 0 --enable-dep-gen
+```
+
+Then preprocess:
+
+```bash
+python tools/agent_pgo_preprocess.py build_output/<case>_<timestamp> \
+  --deps-json outputs/<case>_<depgen_ts>/deps.json \
+  -o experiments/<run>/agent_pgo
+```
+
+If no matching `deps.json` exists, omit `--deps-json`; the profile will still
+emit timing-based tail and long-task fallbacks, but it will not claim structural
+dependency criticality.
+
+Agent read order:
+
+1. `coverage`
+2. `swimlane_morphology`
+3. `causal_story`
+4. `top_functions`
+5. `agent_next_steps`
+6. `agent_pgo_tasks.jsonl` for targeted task lookup
+7. raw L2/PMU/Perfetto artifacts only on demand
+
+Patch priority:
+
+1. Fix swimlane topology first: lane underfill, large idle gaps, fragmented
+   wavefronts, long tail waves, or dependency critical path.
+2. Then fix scheduler/task granularity: too many tiny tasks, dispatch cost,
+   completion cost, sparse ready queue.
+3. Then tune local kernel bodies using PMU: MTE traffic, cube/vector/scalar
+   pressure, instruction/cache symptoms.
+
+Patch retention rule:
+
+```bash
+python tools/agent_pgo_compare.py \
+  experiments/baseline/agent_pgo_profile.json \
+  experiments/candidate/agent_pgo_profile.json \
+  -o experiments/candidate_delta
+```
+
+Keep a patch only when the delta supports the intended hypothesis. For example,
+a topology patch should improve wall time, effective lane utilization,
+tail-wave share, or critical-path time. A PMU patch should improve the targeted
+function counters without making the swimlane topology worse.
+
+Do not stop after the first candidate. Treat `agent_pgo_delta.json` as the
+handoff to the next iteration:
+
+```text
+profile -> hypothesis -> candidate -> measured delta -> keep/reject/rework
+```
+
+Record both kernel run time and tool time for every iteration. The experiment
+runner in `experiments/tool_effect_real_run_20260610/run_profile_case.py` is a
+minimal example: it captures the real command, generated `WORK_DIR`,
+preprocess/compare time, and exit code in `run_records.jsonl`.
+
+## agent_pgo_preprocess.py
+
+Preprocesses runtime DFX artifacts into compact agent-facing PGO evidence.
+It accepts either a `build_output/<case>` directory or its `dfx_outputs/`
+subdirectory, then joins L2 swimlane records and PMU rows by `task_id`.
+It also compresses swimlane topology into lane utilization, idle gaps,
+wavefronts, dependency critical paths, morphology tags, and causal stories.
+
+Recommended capture:
+
+```bash
+python <case>.py -p a2a3 -d 0 --enable-l2-swimlane --enable-pmu 2
+python tools/agent_pgo_preprocess.py build_output/<case>_<timestamp> -o experiments/<run>/agent_pgo
+```
+
+When dependency criticality matters, pass a matching dep-gen artifact:
+
+```bash
+python tools/agent_pgo_preprocess.py build_output/<case>_<timestamp> \
+  --deps-json outputs/<case>_<depgen_ts>/deps.json \
+  -o experiments/<run>/agent_pgo
+```
+
+Outputs:
+
+- `agent_pgo_profile.json` — first file an agent should read
+- `agent_pgo_functions.json` — per-kernel/function aggregates
+- `agent_pgo_tasks.jsonl` — joined task-level evidence for targeted lookup
+- `agent_pgo_findings.md` — concise findings and recommended next steps
+- `artifact_manifest.json` — raw artifact paths used for the profile
+
+Key profile sections:
+
+- `swimlane_morphology` — topology tags such as lane underfill, idle gaps,
+  fragmented wavefronts, long tails, and critical-path dominance
+- `causal_story` — observation/hypothesis/action/validation records for patch
+  selection
+- `l2_global.timeline_analysis` and `l2_global.wavefront_analysis` — compact
+  numeric summaries of the swimlane shape
+- `l2_global.dependency_analysis` — critical path when matching `deps.json` is
+  available, timing fallback otherwise
+
+See `docs/agent_pgo_preprocessing.md` for the data model and interpretation
+rules.
+
+## agent_pgo_compare.py
+
+Compares two `agent_pgo_profile.json` files and emits an agent-facing delta
+report. Use it after a patch to decide whether the change should be kept. The
+delta includes wall time, PMU cycles, function summaries, lane utilization,
+effective concurrency, tail-wave share, and critical-path time when available.
+
+```bash
+python tools/agent_pgo_compare.py \
+  experiments/baseline/agent_pgo_profile.json \
+  experiments/candidate/agent_pgo_profile.json \
+  -o experiments/candidate_delta
+```
+
+Outputs:
+
+- `agent_pgo_delta.json`
+- `agent_pgo_delta.md`
+
+## Small Raw-vs-Tool Comparison
+
+The checked-in probe run under
+`build_output/HelloWorldProgram_20260609_175018/dfx_outputs` is intentionally
+tiny, but it shows the difference between using raw artifacts and using the
+agent profile.
+
+Raw artifacts:
+
+| File | Size | Directly visible |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `l2_swimlane_records.json` | 5,128 bytes | 8 tasks, per-task timing, scheduler/orchestrator phase records |
+| `pmu.csv` | 751 bytes | 8 PMU rows and counters |
+| `merged_swimlane_*.json` | 24,563 bytes | 112 Chrome trace events for Perfetto |
+
+Tool output:
+
+| File | Size | Added value |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `agent_pgo_profile.json` | 33,594 bytes | joined L2+PMU profile, lane/wave/dependency analysis, morphology, causal story |
+| `agent_pgo_findings.md` | 8,989 bytes | concise human/agent summary |
+| `agent_pgo_tasks.jsonl` | 7,913 bytes | joined task rows for targeted retrieval |
+
+Concrete result from the same run:
+
+- raw files expose 8 L2 tasks and 8 PMU rows, but do not directly state an
+  optimization hypothesis.
+- the tool joins those rows by `task_id` and emits 3 swimlane morphology tags:
+  `large_idle_gap`, `long_tail_wave`, and `dependency_graph_missing`.
+- the tool emits 4 causal-story records and 7 next-step recommendations.
+- it reduces the 112 trace events to a compact merged-trace overview, including
+  `duration_event_count=35`, without feeding the full Perfetto trace to the
+  agent by default.
+- it computes topology metrics that are absent from the raw tables:
+  avg lane utilization `0.8726`, effective lane utilization `0.8726`,
+  max concurrency `8`, tail-wave share `1.0`, wall time `76.62 us`.
+- it aggregates PMU counters into action tags: `pmu_total_cycles=786434`,
+  `mte_busy_cycles=714793`, `mte_share=0.9089`, and identifies the top function
+  as `add_scalar_incore_0` with `mte_dominant` pressure. The profile still
+  frames this as local tuning after topology evidence is considered.
+
+## Real Optimization Effect
+
+`experiments/tool_effect_real_run_20260610/` contains real `a2a3` closed-loop
+tests on three kernels: matmul, RMSNorm, and Softmax. This is the more important
+experiment than the raw-vs-tool structure comparison because it measures whether
+tool evidence changes optimization decisions.
+
+Current recorded summary:
+
+| Kernel | Best measured decision | Baseline wall us | Best wall us | Net effect |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| matmul | keep reworked `128x128 chunk1` | 110.78 | 69.06 | -37.66% |
+| RMSNorm | keep combined `row_chunk=32 hidden_chunk=128` | 50.68 | 38.78 | -23.48% |
+| Softmax | reject row-chunk changes; keep baseline | 33.30 | 33.30 | 0.00% |
+
+The run also records optimization overhead: 12 real runs took `127.499 s`
+end-to-end, of which `1.921 s` was preprocess+compare tooling. See
+`experiments/tool_effect_real_run_20260610/results_summary.md` and
+`run_records.jsonl` for the exact commands and timings.
+
+Observed behavior:
+
+- Matmul: the tool exposed `underfilled_hardware_lanes`; the first widening
+  candidate helped wall time but regressed PMU cycles, and a PMU-informed rework
+  became the best candidate.
+- RMSNorm: the first topology candidate over-split tasks and was rejected; the
+  delta made the moderate rework obvious, and combining it with PMU tuning won.
+- Softmax: topology widening was measured and rejected; the best optimization
+  decision was to stop changing `row_chunk`.
+
+The important lesson is not that the first tool-guided candidate always wins.
+The tool is useful because it makes the optimization loop falsifiable:
+
+```text
+trace shape -> causal hypothesis -> candidate patch -> structured delta -> keep/reject/rework
+```
+
+For agent PGO, that loop is more valuable than dumping raw Perfetto traces or
+PMU rows into the agent context.
+
 ## export_all_kernel_insight.py
 
 Exports MindStudio Insight / msprof op-simulator traces for every generated
